        "answer": "GitHub will not automatically disable forks when it disables a parent repository, because forks belong to different users and may have been altered significantly; GitHub also does not conduct any independent investigation into forks [S1]. Instead, GitHub expects the requester to investigate forks and expressly include them in the removal request if they believe the forks also contain the private information [S1].\n\nIf the requester identified all existing forks of the repository at the time of the notice, GitHub would process a valid claim against all forks in that network when processing the notice, given the likelihood that newly created forks would contain the same content [S1]. Additionally, if the reported network contains more than 100 repositories and would be difficult to review in its entirety, GitHub may consider disabling the entire network if the notice states that, based on a representative number of forks reviewed, the requester believes all or most forks contain the reported content [S1].",
